use ./lib/Type

from ../operators/comparison import COMPARISON_OPERATORS
from ../operators/concatenation/ConcatenationOperator import
    ConcatenationOperator
from ../operators/concatenation/DupMergeOperator import DupMergeOperator
from ./Escaping import Escaping

export type Strings:
    description:
        Strings are not quoted. They can appear on the same line as what they’re
        assigned to, or indented on the next line:

        ```piton
        \\\
        sameLine: Hello, World!
        nextLine:
            Hello, World!
        \\\
        ```

        The leading whitespace on a string block is discarded, as that’s part of
        the syntax of the language, not the string. In other words, the string
        in both examples is “Hello, World!”.

    quotes:
        Quotes don't mean anything special. They're just characters, so
        `\ greeting: "Hello" \` is the string `"Hello"`, quotes and all.

        The one exception is inside an expression. There, bare words are
        symbols, so you write strings in double quotes:
        `\ {"Hello" + name} \`. In that case the quotes aren't part of the
        string.

    lineBreaks:
        Within a string block, you can add line breaks without affecting the
        structure of the string. To start a new paragraph, you must include a
        blank line. More than one blank line in a row still counts as one.

        ```piton
        \\\
        myString:
            This broken string is not considered
            a line break.

            While this *is* a new paragraph because there was a blank line above.
        \\\
        ```

        When compiled, we’ll get two paragraphs:

        ```markdown
        \\\
        This broken string is not considered a line break.

        While this *is* a new paragraph because there was a blank line above.
        \\\
        ```

    escaping: @{Escaping}
    codeBlocks:
        Code blocks are not escaped. To Piton they're just text, so anything
        inside them still gets parsed: expressions, comments, lists, all of it.

        So often, you'll want to put an escape block inside the code block.
        Every example in this spec does that.

    markdown:
        Markdown in a string is text too. Piton has no link syntax of its own,
        so a link or an image written in Markdown, like
        `\ [the guide](./guide.md) \` or `\ ![A diagram](./diagram.png) \`, is
        passed through exactly as written. Nothing resolves it, rewrites it, or
        checks that it goes anywhere, and it can never be an error.

        The only links Piton makes are references, `\ @{Button} \`. Those are
        resolved to wherever the anchor was compiled, and a renderer or
        framework can check that they land there.

    supportedOperators:
        - {ConcatenationOperator}
        - {DupMergeOperator}
        + {COMPARISON_OPERATORS}
